{
  "dom_id": "step-e2281",
  "name": "Example: Approximating Ratios by Rounding to the Nearest Ten",
  "problem": "The following table shows the number of children in a park who like baseball.\n\n| Children who like baseball | Children who don't like baseball |\n| --- | --- |\n| $43$ | $69$ |\n\nRounding to the nearest ten, find the approximate ratio of children who like baseball to those who don't.",
  "worked_solution": "First, let's approximate the number of children who like baseball and those who don't like it by rounding to the nearest ten.\n\n- Children who like baseball: $43 \\longrightarrow 40$\n\n- Children who don't like baseball: $69 \\longrightarrow 70$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of children who like baseball to those who don't is approximately\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40}{70} \\, .\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this ratio by dividing the numerator and denominator by $10\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{40 \\div 10}{70 \\div 10} = \\dfrac{4}{7}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ratio of children who like baseball to those who don't is approximately $\\dfrac{4}{7},$ or $4:7.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
